## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/logger.py`

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `DEFAULT_SESSION_DIR = "sessions"` | same |
| `initialize(session_id: nil, dir: nil, log: nil, snapshot: {})` | `def __init__(self, *, session_id=None, dir=None, log=None, snapshot=None)` — **`snapshot=None` then `or {}`**, never a mutable default |
| `FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(@path))` | `Path(self.path).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` |
| `File.open(@path, "a")` | `open(self.path, "a", encoding="utf-8")` |
| `write_log({ phase: "session_start" }.merge(snapshot))` | same construction order — snapshot keys land **after** `phase` |
| `@log_io.puts JSON.generate(event.merge(session_id:, at:))` | `write(json.dumps(..., separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")` then `flush()` |
| `SecureRandom.hex(4)` | `secrets.token_hex(4)` |
| `Time.now.utc.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")` | `datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")` |
| `@log_io&.close` | `close()` guarding a `None` handle |

Public methods, ported exactly as they are in the code (**not** as the README
describes them): `iteration(n:, max:)`, `limit_reached(kind:, n:, max:)`,
`turn_end(reason:, iterations:, tokens: nil)`, `prompt(messages:, tools:)`,
`tool_call(name:, args:)`, `tool_result(name:, result:, ok: true, error: nil)`,
`response(text:, usage: nil, stop_reason: nil, task: nil, backend: nil)`,
`raw(data:)`, `close`.

> ⚠️ **`Time.now.iso8601` has no fractional seconds.** Ruby emits
> `"2026-08-01T12:50:16+01:00"`. Python's `datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat()`
> emits `...:16.123456+01:00` and will not match. Use
> `.isoformat(timespec="seconds")`. Verified against both real session logs.

> ⚠️ **`.compact` applies to `execution_metadata` only, not to the event hashes.**
> `tool_result` deliberately writes `"error": null` when there is no error, and
> `turn_end` writes `"tokens": null` — both confirmed in the real logs. Only the
> `response` event's task/provider/model/usage block drops its nils. A blanket
> "strip None values" helper in Python would silently change three event types.

> ⚠️ **Key order is part of the output.** Ruby hashes and Python dicts both
> preserve insertion order, so building the dicts in Ruby's order gives a
> byte-identical line — but only if you do it deliberately. `response` is
> `phase, text, usage, stop_reason, task, provider, model, usage_unit,
> usage_level, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost_usd, session_id, at`, with
> `session_id` and `at` appended last by the writer.

Private helpers:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `default_dir` → `File.join(Boukensha.config.dir, DEFAULT_SESSION_DIR)` | see **OQ 1** for how to reach module state without a circular import |
| `serialize_message(msg)` → `{ role:, content: }` | same; Python's `Message.role` is already a string, Ruby's symbol serializes to the same JSON |
| `task_name(task)` | **see the trap below** |
| `provider_name(backend)` | `re.sub(r"([a-z\d])([A-Z])", r"\1_\2", type(backend).__name__).lower()` — drop the `split("::")`, Python has no namespace in `__name__`. Gives `anthropic`, `open_ai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `ollama_cloud`, matching Ruby |
| `usage_tokens` / `first_integer(hash, *keys)` | keep the multi-key fallback list (`input_tokens`, `prompt_tokens`, `promptTokenCount`, `prompt_eval_count`); **drop the `key.to_sym` half** — Python JSON dicts have string keys only |
| `Integer(value)` … `rescue ArgumentError, TypeError` | `int(value)` in `try/except (ValueError, TypeError)` — note Ruby raises `ArgumentError`, Python raises `ValueError` |
| `estimate_cost` guard: `return nil unless tokens[:input] && tokens[:output]` | **`is None`, not truthiness** — a genuine `0` token count is truthy in Ruby, falsy in Python. Same class of bug as step 03's `estimate_cost` |

> ⚠️ **`task.task_name` is a method call in Python, an attribute-style call in
> Ruby.** Ruby's `task&.respond_to?(:task_name) ? task.task_name : task&.to_s`
> returns the string. The literal Python transcription
> `task.task_name if hasattr(task, "task_name") else ...` returns a **bound
> method object**, which `json.dumps` then raises `TypeError` on. It must be
> `task.task_name()`. `Tasks::Player.task_name` is a `classmethod` in Python, so
> `hasattr` is `True` and the bug is reached on the very first `response` event.
> Expected value in the log: `"player"`.

### ✏️ Edit — `boukensha/agent.py`

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `logger: Logger.new` default arg | `logger=None` in the signature, `self.logger = logger or Logger()` in the body — Python evaluates defaults once at def-time, so a literal `logger=Logger()` would share one file handle across every Agent ever constructed |
| `puts "[iteration …]"` | `self.logger.iteration(n=…, max=…)` — **the print is deleted, not kept alongside** |
| `puts "  tool call → …"` / `"  tool result → …"` | `self.logger.tool_call(...)` / `.tool_result(...)`; both prints deleted |
| `@logger.limit_reached(...)` before `wrap_up` | same, before the `_wrap_up` return |
| `@logger.prompt(messages:, tools:)` after incrementing | same |
| `@logger.raw(data: response)` after the call | same |
| `handle_tool_calls(parsed[:content], response)` | `_handle_tool_calls(content, response)` — the raw response is threaded through so the assistant turn can be logged with its usage |
| `rescue StandardError => e` around `dispatch` | `except Exception as e:` — result becomes `f"ERROR: {type(e).__name__}: {e}"`, logged with `ok=False` |
| `content.select { \|b\| b["type"] == "tool_use" }` hoisted to `tool_calls` | hoist it the same way; it is used twice (count in the log line, then the dispatch loop) |
| `"(tool use — #{n} call#{'s' if n != 1})"` | `f"(tool use — {n} call{'s' if n != 1 else ''})"` — **em dash, and the pluralization inverts**: Ruby's `'s' if n != 1` yields `nil` (empty) when false, Python's conditional expression needs the explicit `else ""` |
| `normalized_usage(response)` | guarded `.get` walk: `usage` → `usageMetadata` → a dict built from `prompt_eval_count` / `eval_count` if present → `None` |

`log_response` is called from three places: the `end_turn` branch, the tool-use
branch, and `wrap_up`. `turn_end` is logged from **four**: `end_turn`
(`reason="completed"`), `wrap_up`'s success path, and `wrap_up`'s `ApiError`
path (which logs `turn_end` but **not** `log_response`, since there is no
response). Ruby's `rescue` wraps the whole method body; Python's existing `try`
wraps only the call and parse. Keep the narrower `try`, but make sure both exit
paths log `turn_end` with the limit reason, not `"completed"`.

`wrap_up` still runs **outside** the counted loop and still does not increment
`iteration` — the logged `iterations` value is therefore the count of *work*
iterations, which is why the real log shows `"iterations": 2` for a two-call run.

